1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,598
I'm Kohei, and this is one pricing engine applied to two markets.

2
00:00:05,756 --> 00:00:12,323
Week one: a developed market with two point four million policy rows. Rank three of fifty-four.

3
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:26,106
Week two: an emerging market with only two years of data, and a test book that is seventy-six percent new business. Our final file scored point one six four six on the public leaderboard.

4
00:00:26,264 --> 00:00:34,399
The approach: keep what transfers from the developed market, re-measure what does not, and test every step in both directions.

5
00:00:34,557 --> 00:00:47,006
And AI works alongside me throughout: I used Claude Code, steered from my phone by voice, to explore the data, write and test code, run experiments, and verify its own work.

6
00:00:48,064 --> 00:00:51,101
So what changed in the new market?

7
00:00:51,259 --> 00:00:55,865
Motor claims are thirty-four percent more frequent, but forty-two percent cheaper.

8
00:00:56,023 --> 00:01:02,197
Glass, third-party and injury claims are far more frequent. Escape of water is far less.

9
00:01:02,355 --> 00:01:07,745
And three out of four test policies are brand-new customers with no history.

10
00:01:07,903 --> 00:01:13,686
We measured that these differences are stable across both years, instead of assuming it.

11
00:01:14,743 --> 00:01:18,957
The method keeps what transfers, and re-measures what does not.

12
00:01:19,115 --> 00:01:27,250
A developed-market model, trained on the variables both markets share, ranks who is risky. I add last year's claim history.

13
00:01:27,408 --> 00:01:34,367
An ensemble of five tree models, seven neural nets and four Tweedie models averages out the noise.

14
00:01:34,525 --> 00:01:42,660
Then the emerging book sets the level: claim counts and winsorised severities for each peril, credibility-weighted, plus one gender factor.

15
00:01:42,818 --> 00:01:47,424
The emerging data decides how much. The developed data decides who.

16
00:01:48,481 --> 00:01:51,911
Here is the structure of the final submission.

17
00:01:52,069 --> 00:02:00,204
Five tree models: LightGBM with four and eight leaves, single and three-seed fits, a column-sampled variant, and an XGBoost model.

18
00:02:00,362 --> 00:02:10,458
Seven neural nets: TabM, TabM with piecewise-linear encoding, and TabM with quantile inputs, each as a single and a three-seed fit, plus one wider TabM.

19
00:02:10,616 --> 00:02:16,398
Four direct Tweedie models complete it, at four and eight leaves, single and three-seed.

20
00:02:16,556 --> 00:02:27,829
Each group is averaged with equal weights: trees and nets thirty-seven and a half percent each, Tweedie twenty-five. Then the emerging level and the gender factor are applied.

21
00:02:28,886 --> 00:02:34,277
With two years of data, a single split can mistake noise for signal.

22
00:02:34,434 --> 00:02:44,139
So every change is tested in both directions, calibrating on 2023 and scoring 2024 and the reverse, and kept only if neither gets worse.

23
00:02:44,296 --> 00:02:52,432
That took the score from point one one one oh, for an emerging-only model, to point one four oh five.

24
00:02:52,589 --> 00:02:58,764
More than thirty ideas were rejected this way, including pooled training and a GLM blend.

25
00:02:58,922 --> 00:03:09,802
A simulation with a known truth confirmed the level method, and a ceiling analysis showed that a perfect level is worth at most half of one hundredth.

26
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:13,897
Here is how that looked in practice.

27
00:03:14,055 --> 00:03:25,328
Claude Code explored both markets, wrote the code with one hundred and twenty-five tests, and ran the experiments, while more than ten helper sessions refit models in parallel.

28
00:03:25,485 --> 00:03:30,484
A blind second agent compared the markets from the raw tables alone.

29
00:03:30,641 --> 00:03:42,306
And verification caught real errors: unsupported statements in a first draft, a simulator bug that looked like a model bias, and a net that Part one had already rejected.

30
00:03:42,464 --> 00:03:45,109
Every number traces to a command.

31
00:03:46,167 --> 00:03:50,381
One example of how this changes the way I work.

32
00:03:50,538 --> 00:04:02,595
Part of week two was done from my phone, on a bus, with no laptop. Claude Code ran in the cloud, and I controlled it remotely from the Claude app.

33
00:04:02,753 --> 00:04:14,026
I gave my instructions by voice. The app transcribes my speech, Claude Code picks it up, runs the work, and reports back while I am on the move.

34
00:04:14,183 --> 00:04:18,005
That is AI-assisted actuarial work, away from the desk.

35
00:04:19,063 --> 00:04:24,453
Guardrails: a leak alarm, reproducibility checks, a model card, and eighty-four recorded decisions.

36
00:04:24,611 --> 00:04:32,354
The biggest uncertainty is the buildings level. Plus or minus twenty percent is about one hundredth of a point.

37
00:04:32,512 --> 00:04:35,942
And gender rating is flagged for regulatory review.

38
00:04:36,099 --> 00:04:40,705
Built with an AI teammate, from a bus seat. Thank you.
